expr main (I'm agree)

let message "`agree` is a verb, so it doesn't need `am`: `I agree`."
let description "Corrects `I'm agree` to `I agree`."
let kind "Grammar"
let becomes "I agree"
let strategy "MatchCase"

# real sentences from Hacker News and GitHub issues
test "C++ templates are ugly, I'm agree, but they are useful and heavily used." "C++ templates are ugly, I agree, but they are useful and heavily used."
test "As CS teacher I'm agree with others that JS is terrible choice as first language." "As CS teacher I agree with others that JS is terrible choice as first language."
# made-up sentences
test "I'm agree with you." "I agree with you."
test "Yes, I'm agree." "Yes, I agree."
test "I'm agree with the plan, but not the timeline." "I agree with the plan, but not the timeline."
test "Honestly, I'm agree on this one." "Honestly, I agree on this one."
test "I'M AGREE." "I AGREE."
test "So I'm agree that we should wait." "So I agree that we should wait."

# correct text
allows "I agree with you."
allows "I'm in agreement with you."
allows "I'm sure you agree."
allows "I agreed with the plan."
# look-alikes that must not be flagged
allows "I'm agreeable to that."
allows "I'm, agree or not, staying."
allows "What I'm agreeing to is the scope."
allows "I'm not sure I agree."
allows "The terms I'm asked to agree to are long."
